CLI Script Specification
========================

Every script in this repo is a self-contained Python 3 file that can be piped
directly into Python (curl | python) or executed locally. This file defines the
structure and conventions all scripts must follow.


File structure
--------------

Every script follows this exact top-to-bottom layout:

    #!/usr/bin/env python3
    """
    Name: One-line description.
    Usage: scriptname [arg1] [arg2]
    """

Scripts are invoked by name, not as Python files. The Usage line must use the
bare script name (e.g. "search", not "python search.py" or "search.py").

    import ...       # stdlib only - no third-party imports at module level
                     # subprocess calls to external tools are fine

    # -- fmt ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    import os as _os; _e = _os.environ
    _C = "NO_COLOR" not in _e and bool(_e.get("FORCE_COLOR")=="1" or _e.get("WT_SESSION") or _e.get("ANSICON") or _e.get("TERM_PROGRAM") or _e.get("TERM","") not in ("","dumb"))
    def section(t): print(f"\n\033[36m=== {t} ===\033[0m" if _C else f"\n=== {t} ===")
    def info(m):    print(f"\033[90m[*]\033[0m {m}"       if _C else f"[*] {m}")
    def ok(m):      print(f"\033[32m[+]\033[0m {m}"       if _C else f"[+] {m}")
    def err(m):     print(f"\033[31m[-]\033[0m {m}"       if _C else f"[-] {m}")
    def warn(m):    print(f"\033[33m[!]\033[0m {m}"       if _C else f"[!] {m}")
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    CONSTANTS = ...      # optional

    def helper(): ...    # implementation functions

    def main():
        section("Script Title")
        ...
        print()          # trailing blank line

    if __name__ == "__main__":
        main()

Copy the fmt block verbatim. Do not modify it, split it across files, or replace
it with imports. It must be identical in every script so that look and feel is
enforced at the source level, not by convention.


Output conventions
------------------

section("Title")     Start of script output and major phases. Prints a blank
                     line before the header. Call once at the top of main(),
                     and again when output naturally splits into sections
                     (e.g. sysinfo uses one per hardware category).

info("msg")          Progress and status during execution. Use for steps the
                     user should know are happening: connecting, scanning,
                     processing. Do not spam - one per logical step.

ok("msg")            Confirms a successful outcome. Use when the script
                     produces a specific artifact or completes a task:
                     "Done. Exported 14 files to dump.txt"

err("msg")           Fatal errors. Always followed by print() and sys.exit(1).
                     The prefix [-] signals that the script has failed.

warn("msg")          Degraded but continuing execution: a tool is missing and
                     a fallback is being tried, a feature is unavailable on
                     this platform, or a query returns no results. Use instead
                     of err() whenever the script does not exit.

print()              Blank line. Always end main() with one. Add between
                     section() and content if the section header needs visual
                     separation. Do not use for padding inside content blocks.

Usage message        When args are wrong or missing, call section() first,
                     then print plain-text usage lines, then print(), then
                     sys.exit(1). No err() prefix on usage lines.


Fatal error pattern
-------------------

    err("Human-readable description of what went wrong")
    print()
    sys.exit(1)

Always print() before sys.exit so the terminal prompt appears on its own line.


Encoding
--------

All source files must be pure ASCII. No Unicode characters in comments,
strings, or identifiers. Use escape sequences for any non-ASCII output.

File operations must declare encoding explicitly:

    open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8")


Dependencies
------------

Scripts must work with Python 3 stdlib only. No pip installs required to run
the basic functionality. External CLI tools (mutool, pandoc, nvidia-smi) are
tried opportunistically via subprocess and degraded gracefully when absent.

When a tool is unavailable, call err() with a short install hint:

    err("mutool not available  (install: apt install mupdf-tools / brew install mupdf)")

Then try the next strategy. Never crash because an optional tool is missing.


Platform handling
-----------------

Use platform.system() to branch on "Windows", "Linux", "Darwin". When a
feature is unavailable on a platform, print an informative message rather
than silently returning nothing.


Adding a new script
-------------------

1. Create the file following the structure above.
2. Add an entry to the SCRIPTS dict in index.html:

       "scriptname": {
           "description": "One sentence, same style as the others.",
           "url": BASE_URL + "scriptname",
           "args": [                          # omit key if no args
               {
                   "name": "argname",
                   "description": "Optional: ..."
               }
           ]
       }
